qsm-pve-direct for Proxmox VE 9
===============================

`qsm-pve-direct` is a browser-native PVE console transport.

Standalone release 0.4.0+stock4.13
-----------------------------
For official PVE 9 install matching qsm-pve-direct and
qsm-pve-direct-standalone debs. Follow README.standalone, installed with the
standalone profile, or INSTALL-STOCK.txt in the release. It uses a configurable
HTTPS signalling port (initially a free port starting at 18007) and direct
WebRTC UDP. Existing core-only configurations can retain port 8007.

The optional qsm-pve-direct-gateway profile shares node TCP 8006 between PVE
HTTPS, QSM signalling and authenticated TURN/TCP. A router/NAT can forward only
that TCP port; no public UDP mapping is needed. Read its README.gateway /
INSTALL-GATEWAY.txt before activation: it changes the proxy listener and has
single-node, custom-proxy and SPICE limitations. HTTPS and WebRTC protect user
session contents, but TURN control is plain TCP WITHOUT TLS. This package does
not supply a TURN/TLS transport. Neither profile needs rebuilt PVE packages.

The LXC helper is installed on the PVE host and embeds the container package.
NEVER install qsm-console-guest directly on the PVE host. Existing patched
native installations can update the core/helper while retaining their matched
Proxmox backend packages; standalone/gateway is a separate planned transition.
Stock3 also preserves shared-display VM console detection under a stock UI
when the native qemu-server backend is retained. See README.standalone.

Native shared platform (patched Proxmox packages)
------------------------------------------------
Install the shared-base and WebRTC patches from upstream/README.md, then
install this package. Stop each VM before changing its display configuration:

    qm set <vmid> --dbus-display 1 --vga std
    qm set <vmid> --audio0 device=ich9-intel-hda,driver=dbus

For VirGL use --vga virtio-gl,rendernode=/dev/dri/renderD128 instead.
For existing QSM-managed VMs, preview and apply the conversion:

    qsm-pve-direct-migrate <vmid>
    qsm-pve-direct-migrate <vmid> --apply

The tool preserves unrelated QEMU arguments and the optional QSM guest
companion, and makes a private configuration backup before applying changes.
Start the VM and reload the PVE browser page. Its Console tab and menu use
WebRTC, with noVNC still available for CPU displays.

qemu-server owns /run/qemu-server/<vmid>.dbusdisplay and its VM lifecycle.
QSM is a consumer; restarting its service does not replace that bus. Other
display consumers may attach independently. Signalling, status and codec
settings use authenticated PVE API routes on port 8006. With the matching
pve-container WebRTC patches, LXC consoles use the same API and the common
PVE::WebRTC Unix-socket transport from pve-guest-common. Neither VM nor LXC
native consoles require port 8007 or a separate certificate exception.
Enable qsm-pve-direct-terminal.service; the separate signal service is only
for legacy add-on installations. WebRTC media still uses negotiated UDP
ports directly between browser and host. VM.Console permits viewing/settings reads;
VM.Config.Options permits settings writes. Clipboard uses qemu-vdagent and
spice-vdagent in X11 guests, or the retained QSM companion for configured
desktop guests. Concurrent clipboard ownership across protocols is not
provided by QEMU's single clipboard proxy.

Standalone add-on mode (unpatched PVE)
------------------------------------

It uses PVE's existing authenticated Web UI session and `VM.Console` ACL.
The browser sends its WebRTC SDP offer to the node-local signalling service
(`qsm-pve-direct-signal`, configured HTTPS port with the node certificate), which
confirms the browser's PVE ticket and `VM.Console` with pveproxy's own
`/access/ticket`; the node-local terminal service answers it and starts one
QEMU Display1 worker.  Negotiated H.264 or HEVC video and Opus remain
encoded until the browser's WebRTC implementation. There is no pairing PIN,
native client installer or host desktop server. Direct UDP needs that HTTPS
port and negotiated UDP; the optional gateway instead shares node TCP 8006.

Install:

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_0.4.0+stock3_amd64.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-standalone_0.4.0+stock3_all.deb
    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --show
    qsm-pve-direct-ui status

The PVE web page gets one script tag (a diverted `index.html.tpl`) only for
the `pve-manager` versions and template checksums the package knows; on any
other version the stock page stays and VMs keep the noVNC console until a
package update adds it.  `qsm-pve-direct-ui status` shows the state.

The service reads PVE's saved VM configuration and creates the private
Display1 bus before the VM is started. No per-VM policy file is required: the
portable `auto` policy prefers HEVC when the current browser offers H.265 and
a tested NVENC/QSV/VA-API HEVC encoder is available, otherwise the H.264 lane
(NVENC, QSV, VA-API, then `libx264`).
After saving a Display1 setting, the service
reconciles the bus automatically. Package upgrades may restart the terminal
service, but preserve every live private Display1 bus: a running configured
VM does not need to be rebooted solely for this package update.

In the PVE VM's **Hardware → Display → Advanced**, enable **QSM Display1**
and select one profile:

* **VirGL GPU (GL)** saves PVE Graphic card as `none`, then owns one private
  `virtio-vga-gl` device and `-display dbus,...,gl=on,rendernode=...`. PVE VNC
  cannot coexist with that GL Display1 backend.
* **CPU — Standard VGA, VirtIO or VMware (no GL)** keeps the selected PVE
  `std`, non-GL `virtio` or `vmware` graphic adapter and writes only
  `-display dbus,...,gl=off`. It needs neither a render node nor a host GPU;
  normal PVE VNC remains usable alongside QSM Direct.

Sound: **Hardware → Add → Audio Device** offers the backend **QSM Console**
on such a VM: an Intel HDA sound card with a microphone input (`hda-micro`)
on a QEMU dbus audiodev
(`-audiodev dbus,id=qsm-direct-audio,...`, 48 kHz stereo both ways, named in
`-display ...,audiodev=qsm-direct-audio`), kept in `args` because PVE's own
audio backends (SPICE, none) cannot feed a D-Bus display; the Hardware list
shows it as the Audio Device, Edit and Remove work on it.  The console starts
muted, as
browsers require; its speaker button turns the sound on, its microphone
button sends the browser's microphone to the guest's microphone input.

Reopening the dialog restores the checkbox, profile, and (for VirGL only)
render node from the saved arguments. The existing VM **Console** entry in the
left navigation (between **Summary** and **Hardware**) opens QSM Direct only
for one of those exact private Display1 configurations; otherwise it remains
the stock noVNC Console. QSM Direct renders in that same PVE Console area.
The QSM Direct entry in the top Console split menu remains an optional
separate-window launch and is enabled under the same condition. Start the VM,
then choose **Console**.
Resizing the console window or
entering/leaving fullscreen sends the selected even viewport to QEMU Display1
and causes a fresh video configuration/IDR boundary when the guest adapter
implements `Console.SetUIInfo`. Non-GL VirtIO does; QEMU Standard VGA exposes
a fixed Display1 scanout and rejects that optional method, so QSM keeps the
video and input connected at its guest-selected mode rather than failing the
console.

The Console window's **Settings** panel stores the media policy per VM on the
node in root-owned `/etc/qsm-pve-direct/instances.d/<VMID>.conf` (0600), not
in browser storage or unsupported `qm.conf` keys. Its choices are
**Automatic** (prefer negotiated hardware HEVC, otherwise probe H.264 with
`libx264` fallback), **Hardware only**
(fail clearly rather than consume CPU when no accelerator works), and
**Software** (`libx264`). Close all QSM Direct windows for the VM and reopen
one after saving: one worker is shared by its active viewers.

HEVC is negotiated when the current browser SDP offers H.265 and the node
initializes a hardware HEVC encoder; the answer repeats the browser's own
H265 payload type and profile/tier/level. Qualified against Chrome on Apple
silicon with `hevc_nvenc`.
This is a browser/WebRTC and server-RTP capability boundary, not a VirGL
limitation. A VM shares one worker, so mixed viewers must support its active
codec or reconnect after the existing Console is closed.

## Performance profile compared with PVE noVNC

This is a transport comparison, not an invented universal millisecond
benchmark: the result depends on guest workload, network loss, browser
decoder, chosen encoder, and display profile. Compare both consoles at the
same guest resolution and refresh rate. QSM's release stress suite records
first-video time, Chrome jitter-buffer delay, key-to-pixel latency, hover
latency, and continuous-drag gaps; noVNC has no equivalent built-in PVE
telemetry, so its numbers must be collected with the same guest fixture before
publishing a hardware-specific result.

| Performance property | QSM Direct | Stock PVE noVNC |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser video path | WebRTC H.264 or negotiated hardware HEVC / Opus; the browser may use its hardware decoder | RFB rectangles over a reliable WebSocket, decoded and painted by noVNC/Canvas |
| Host work for changing pixels | One low-latency H.264 or HEVC encode per VM worker, shared by its viewers; hardware encoders are preferred by `Automatic` | QEMU VNC/RFB rectangle updates; no video encoder or browser video-decoder path |
| Pointer behaviour under congestion | Latest pointer samples use an unordered, non-retransmitted WebRTC channel, so stale motion is discarded | Pointer events share the ordered reliable WebSocket stream; a delayed stream cannot skip earlier events |
| Video under packet loss | Media can recover at the next video intra frame while input keeps its separate control path | TCP/WebSocket head-of-line recovery delays later RFB updates behind a missing packet |
| Resize | One even Display1 mode request followed by a video configuration/IDR boundary | Rescales or redraws the current RFB framebuffer; it does not request a QEMU Display1 guest mode |
| Best fit | Interactive desktop, animation, video, and high-latency or lossy links | Firmware text console, installation, recovery, and a zero-extra-transport baseline |

For a reproducible QSM result, run the documented
`run-qsm-direct-stress-suite.py` from the PVE node and retain its single JSON
record with the browser/guest topology. Do not compare its result with a
noVNC value gathered at a different guest geometry, browser, or network path.

An administrator may still create a root-owned policy file directly to pin a
specific node encoder without changing the display setup:

    QSM_DIRECT_QEMU_DBUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/qsm-pve-direct/<VMID>/qemu-display1.bus
    QSM_DIRECT_ENCODER=h264_nvenc

To pin HEVC, pair a hardware encoder with `QSM_DIRECT_CODEC=hevc`, for example:

    QSM_DIRECT_CODEC=hevc
    QSM_DIRECT_ENCODER=hevc_nvenc

For VA-API additionally set
`QSM_DIRECT_VAAPI_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128`.

The console Settings panel saves **Maximum video bitrate (Mbit/s)** per VM
or LXC on the node. The range is 0.5–100 Mbit/s; existing installations default
to 8 Mbit/s. This requires the updated `webrtcsettings` API packages
(qemu-server 9.2.11+shared3 / pve-container 6.1.13+webrtc2 in this preview).
Reads retain VM.Console authorization; changing it requires VM.Config.Options.
Close all viewers for that guest and reconnect to replace its shared encoder.
A VM/container restart or guest package update is not required.

The equivalent root-owned policy setting is:

    QSM_DIRECT_BITRATE_KBPS=20000

Use instances.d/<VMID>.conf for VMs or containers.d/<VMID>.conf for LXC.
The value is in decimal kbit/s (500–100000), and survives codec changes and
re-running the LXC setup helper. The encoder rate/VBV budget and RTP pacing
are derived from the same value; the pacer allows 25% overhead/recovery
headroom and a bounded burst. Audio and packet/network overhead are additional
to the configured video rate. With NVENC, software H.264, QSV or VA-API this
is the ceiling for automatic congestion control. The worker preserves its
configured encoder settings while adaptation changes the budget for admitting
raw pictures. Limited bandwidth reduces motion cadence; static reference
detail is retained. Complete encoded pictures are never dropped merely to
meet that budget. Explicit media timestamps preserve timing at lower cadence.

The supported in-process encoders request keyframes for joining/recovering viewers,
rather than forcing a full refresh every half second. This avoids periodic
blurring of static text under the low-latency VBV budget. Local recovery
requests retry at most once a second until an IDR arrives. A network target
change itself does not reset the codec or force a new full refresh.

QSV and VA-API use the same raw-frame budget, without reopening their codec
on network target changes. Devices are opened before an LXC worker drops
root; encoding continues as nobody without changing render-node permissions.
The earlier Intel/AMD codec qualifications are linked in the project README;
the new admission/timestamp path has separate validation. Existing API
shared3/webrtc2 packages remain compatible.
For pve-container 6.1.14 use its matching 6.1.14+webrtc2 package, rather than
installing the 6.1.13 variant.

The direct WebRTC console provides video, guest audio and microphone (with a
QSM Console Audio Device), keyboard, pointer, scrolling, dynamic Display1
resolution, and optional guest clipboard integration. For clipboard install the matching `qsm-desktop-agent` package
inside the Linux VM, then run `sudo qsm-desktop-agent-setup USER` once for its
logged-in desktop user. On Wayland, also enable `qsm-desktop-clipboard.service`
in that user's session.

The browser's **Copy** button copies the guest's current plain-text selection
to the local system clipboard; `Ctrl/Cmd+C` first sends `Ctrl+C` to the focused
Linux guest application, then copies the resulting selection. **Paste** and
`Ctrl/Cmd+V` read the browser clipboard, update the guest selection, then send
`Shift+Insert` to the focused guest application. Browser security requires this
explicit user gesture; a guest clipboard update cannot silently overwrite the
host system clipboard.

LXC container consoles
----------------------

A container has no QEMU display.  QSM Direct runs the container's desktop
session (KDE Plasma, Wayland or X11) inside a headless sway ("the display")
in the container; the host media worker captures that display (wlr-screencopy)
and injects input (virtual pointer/keyboard), using the host GPU encoder.
The worker resolves the display socket inside the container's root (symlinks
cannot redirect it to a host socket) and drops to "nobody" once connected.

The container console is the package qsm-pve-direct-lxc (the setup tool); the
container side is the package qsm-console-guest, which the tool installs into
the container with apt.

Enable a running Debian/Ubuntu container that already has a Plasma desktop:

    qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable <vmid> [--session wayland|x11] [--grace SECONDS] [--restart]

This passes the GPU through (NVIDIA device nodes and the DRM render node;
--restart applies it), installs NVIDIA userspace matching the host kernel
module from the host's NVIDIA-Linux-x86_64-<version>.run (--nvidia-run FILE),
installs the container package qsm-console-guest with apt (pulling in sway,
xwayland, PyQt5, acl), which runs the container's consoles as system
services -- one per tty the container runs with (its "tty" option, PVE
default 2), like PVE's terminal console:

    qsm-consoles.service       at boot: starts one console per /dev/ttyN
    qsm-display@N.service      headless sway (user qsm-display), /run/qsm-display-N
    qsm-seat-keeper@N.service  keeps the display's input devices stable
    qsm-login@N.service        login manager: login screen, PAM, the session
    qsm-session-N.service      (transient) one logged-in desktop session

Every PVE Console gets its own console and starts at its login screen; no
Console ever shows another Console's desktop.  Like a tty in use, a console
belongs to the PVE user who opened it: when that Console is closed with a
desktop still logged in, the desktop waits --grace seconds (default 300) for
the same PVE user -- who then gets it back -- and is logged out after that.
A left login screen is freed at once.  When all consoles are in use, the
browser says so.  The count follows
the container's tty option by itself: like for the terminal console, a changed
value applies at the container's next start (host and container both read
the running value).  One container account can have one console desktop at a
time (two Plasma sessions of one user break each other).

The original login profile supplies no graphical logind seat and disables
the ordinary display manager in favour of qsm-login. The optional native
SDDM profile supplies dedicated VKMS/logind seats without host VTs; see
the LXC package README.native-sddm for activation and qualification.
The login screen lists the container's accounts (new ones appear live); pick
one, enter its password, choose Plasma (Wayland) or Plasma (X11).  qsm-login
checks the password with PAM (service qsm-login) and starts the session
through systemd with PAMName=qsm-direct-session, i.e. a real logind session
on seat0 (polkit dialogs, user manager, limits work as after a normal
desktop login; with several desktops only the newest is "active" to polkit).
Log Out returns to the login screen.  Clipboard, sound and microphone work
without setup: every desktop session runs qsm-clipboard-agent, which bridges
Klipper (the same on Wayland and X11) and gives the session a "QSM Console"
sound card and a "QSM Microphone" as its default devices (pipewire-pulse
pipe sink/source; a container has no sound hardware).  The host reaches both
over sockets in /run/qsm-login/clip-N that only the logged-in user can reach;
the media worker encodes the session's sound, and the browser's microphone
plays into the session's microphone.  Only the current client of a console's
display -- its login screen or its logged-in user -- may connect to it (an
ACL on the display socket, like logind's uaccess).  The host asks a console's
login manager for its state or a logout over a root-only socket in the
container (/run/qsm-login/N/control), opened without following symlinks.

A desktop user "qsm" (--user/--uid) is created if missing and added to the
sudo group (polkit administrators; skip with --no-admin).  Any account of the
container with a password can log in; set one:

    pct exec <vmid> -- passwd qsm

enable writes /etc/qsm-pve-direct/containers.d/<vmid>.conf.  The container's
Console button then offers "QSM Direct" to users with VM.Console on
/vms/<vmid>; its Console tab keeps the stock xterm.js terminal.

    qsm-pve-direct-lxc session <vmid> wayland|x11   default session of the login screen
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc status  <vmid>
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc disable <vmid>

Notes: the in-session screen locker's auto-lock is disabled (console access is
already gated by PVE) and KScreen's automatic mode selection is off (the
console window decides the resolution).  X11 clients render through SHM: the
headless display offers no DRM device for Xwayland's glamor.

Native LXC SDDM profile (opt-in)
------------------------------
qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable supports --login-manager sddm --sddm-deb FILE
--restart. Dedicated virtual graphical seats let a full SDDM authenticate
and own desktop sessions without host VTs. See the LXC package
README.native-sddm for the qualified Ubuntu/Qt 5 profile and rollback.

Keyboard reliability (stock4.2)
------------------------------
Reliable delivery can still delay keyup long enough to start native guest
autorepeat. Exact typing is now the default: each source press or repeat is
one complete key_tap action, including its modifier snapshot. The worker
executes its down/up edges locally, so network timing cannot stretch that
action. The first character does not wait for a heartbeat. Repeats are sent
only for real browser repeat events, never synthesized from elapsed network
time. Clipboard and special-key chords use the same atomic command.

Physical holds and Raw input retain native guest-held state for games and
shortcut editors. Raw always forces physical holds. A per-peer state snapshot
arrives every 50 ms; a 200 ms watchdog bounds unconfirmed holds. This watchdog
is a fallback, not a guarantee against all false native repeats: a recent
heartbeat can keep a key held across the native repeat threshold. Exact typing
is the mode that avoids this transport-induced count error. A network stall
can cancel a physical hold; release and press again to resume it.

Closing a console releases only its own input, even if another viewer keeps
the shared producer alive. Atomic actions preserve previously held modifiers
and do not release another viewer's held main key.
Reload the PVE page with cache bypass and reopen consoles after upgrading.
Old cached viewers retain the older edge-only behavior. Health records expose
input_leased, input_snapshots and timeout/timing counters without logging key
identities or typed content. Client/server hold timing distinguishes browser
event delay from delayed delivery.

Guest-first/Raw captures available browser keys in fullscreen. Stock4.4
supports Firefox/Safari via the Fullscreen API option, and Chromium via its
separate Keyboard Lock API. Permission may be required. Console Settings
retains Send to guest buttons for Esc, Alt+Tab, Ctrl+Alt+Del and Super/Windows.

Mouse / trackpad input (stock4.3)
--------------------------------
Exact clicks is the default for ordinary mouse clicks, trackpad taps/clicks
and touchscreen taps. A short click is one complete action sent on release,
including position and modifiers. Moving 4 CSS pixels or holding for 250 ms
starts a physical hold/drag; its motion/release stay ordered until the bridge
acknowledges the release. A 50 ms snapshot renews held state, and 200 ms without
confirmation releases it without re-pressing from stale queued snapshots.
Physical button holds or Raw sends down immediately. Touch hold-to-drag stays
at 500 ms. Both scroll axes work and fractional trackpad deltas accumulate.
Normal double clicks pass; native double-click timing can still be affected
by delivery jitter. Generic virtual input cannot carry original click times.
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen consoles. No guest update is needed.

Fullscreen keyboard capture (stock4.4)
-------------------------------------
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen the console after updating.
Click the text Capture keyboard button near the top of the left toolbar
or the first action in Settings. Guest first alone is a routing policy.
The embedded console enters actual full screen for capture; its existing
page-maximize button still provides in-page enlargement separately.
Capture selects Guest first if Client first was saved, preserving Exact typing
and Exact clicks. Firefox desktop 151+ uses requestFullscreen keyboardLock:
"browser"; Safari uses the same API where supported. Chromium retains
navigator.keyboard.lock() and its permission flow. F11 is not required, so
macOS system assignments need no changes. Click the guest picture for input.

A short Esc is sent to the guest. Hold Esc or click Release keyboard to exit.
Switching to Client first or closing the console releases capture.
Opening Settings preserves fullscreen and releases guest-held input; close
Settings to focus the guest and resume capture. Stock4.5 pauses input on host app/tab switching without exiting
fullscreen, then resumes capture inside the existing fullscreen on return.
Held keys/buttons are released on blur; old holds are never restored. Guest first reserves Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Esc;
Raw keeps its existing no-QSM-chord policy. OS-reserved keys are not guaranteed
to reach any web app; use Send to guest buttons where necessary.

Headful Firefox 157 / Linux XTEST / real LXC checks cover ordinary Esc and
browser chords in separate and embedded consoles; complete production UI
smoke checks pass too. No physical macOS/Safari qualification is claimed.

Logical modifiers and host switching (stock4.6)
---------------------------------------------
Use Guest first to respect host modifier remapping (including a macOS
Option/Command swap). Printable positions still follow the guest layout.
Logical Command+Tab / Command+Shift+Tab stay on the Mac and are not sent to
the guest. Logical Option+Tab sends guest Alt+Tab. With swapped host keys,
physical Option now means Command (host switch), and physical Command now
means Option (guest switch). A browser cannot override native macOS app
switching or a third-party global shortcut; do not promise exclusive capture
of those bindings. The existing Alt+Tab Send to guest button stays available.

Outside Raw on macOS, Command is deferred until a guest action, avoiding a
stray guest Alt/Super/menu effect from host Command+Tab. Complete keyboard
and exact mouse actions include logical modifiers. Physical main-key holds,
physical dragging and modifier-wheel input flush deferred Command as needed.
A genuine solo Command tap still sends Super, while blur/host routing cancel
it. Late source presses while unfocused and foreign repeats are ignored.
Raw deliberately uses physical positions/edges and bypasses host-only routing;
use it only when literal physical input is required. Reload PVE without cache
and reopen consoles after upgrading. No guest update is required.

Settings and capture controls (stock4.7)
---------------------------------------
Opening/editing Settings releases guest-held input while preserving full
screen. Close at the top right refocuses the guest and resumes capture.
Capture keyboard is a text action near the top of the left toolbar and the
first Settings action, visible without scrolling. Reopening Settings resets
its scroll position. Guest first alone selects input routing; Capture keyboard
requests the browser's full-screen capture. The separate embedded in-page
maximize control remains available. The reveal handle sits beside a visible
toolbar, avoiding overlap with its controls at smaller iframe sizes.
Explicit Release keyboard, Client first, close and long Esc remain exits.
Reload the PVE page and reopen consoles after updating; no guest update.

Fixed controls and one-way capture action (stock4.8)
--------------------------------------------------
The controls handle stays at the left screen edge when toggled. The toolbar
has its own inset, preserving access without overlap or moving the handle
according to toolbar width. The handle's accessible state follows visibility.
Capture keyboard now only enables/resumes capture; repeated clicks keep
fullscreen, including from Settings and an existing ordinary fullscreen.
Release keyboard in Settings is a separate explicit action. Exit Full Screen
also exits native capture in embedded and separate consoles. Outside native
fullscreen, the embedded in-page maximize keeps its existing behavior.
The button label stays Capture keyboard; its pressed state marks the request.
Reload the PVE page and reopen consoles after updating; no guest update.

Deferred host-shortcut prefixes (stock4.9)
---------------------------------------
Stock4.9 extends deferred shortcut prefixes to Alt, Ctrl and Shift as well
as Command. A host can consume Tab before DOM; pending modifiers then
cancel on focus loss without a stray guest Alt/menu press. Exact typing
sends modifiers inside the complete guest action. Solo modifiers and
modifier-only shortcuts complete on focused release. Physical main-key
holds, physical dragging and wheel input flush pending modifiers before
the guest action; Raw retains immediate physical edges. No debounce is
added to an ordinary key press. Matching modern host capability is required.

Configurable host shortcuts (stock4.10)
-------------------------------------
Stock4.10 replaces fixed host/browser exceptions with editable Host
shortcuts in Settings > Keyboard shortcut priority. Each rule has an exact
logical modifier combination and a Host/browser, Release keyboard, Toggle
fullscreen or Show/hide controls action. Add, record, edit, remove or reset
rules; preferences apply immediately and persist in this browser. Guest first
and Client first have separate lists. Raw bypasses the list. Default host
rules reserve Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab, Meta+Tab and Shift+Meta+Tab; Ctrl+Alt+
Shift+Escape releases capture. A per-rule Send to guest bypass is available.
No additional Ctrl+Alt+Tab remapping is installed. Host rules prevent guest
forwarding; native OS/browser execution remains subject to its own policy.

Reserved shortcut prefixes (stock4.11)
--------------------------------------
Stock4.11 fixes a remaining host-shortcut prefix leak: the OS can consume
Tab and deliver Alt/Meta keyup before blur while the page still has focus.
Modifier-only prefixes of an active Host shortcuts rule now stay local on
release; they cannot turn a host switch into a solo guest Alt/Super/menu tap.
This also means a deliberate bare reserved modifier uses the explicit Alt,
Alt+Shift or Super Send to guest button. Unreserved modifier-only actions,
normal guest chords such as Alt+F, explicit per-rule sends and Raw remain
available. The active editable rule list controls which prefixes are local.

Exact modifier-only shortcut rules (stock4.12)
---------------------------------------------
Stock4.12 makes completed modifier-only combinations explicit shortcut
rules. All 11 combinations of two or more Ctrl/Alt/Shift/Meta modifiers
are listed as Host/browser by default in both profiles. Existing browser
preferences gain missing defaults once, preserving custom actions. Remove
a rule or select Guest to send that exact combination immediately on first
release; later releases do not add taps. Deleted defaults stay deleted on
reload. Alt+Shift, Ctrl+Shift and Win+Shift no longer remain blocked as
prefixes of longer host rules after their exact rule is removed. Recording,
editing and Send to guest support modifier-only combinations. Main-key
chords are matched separately. Raw and bare host-prefix protection remain.

Modifier press order (stock4.13)
--------------------------------
Stock4.13 preserves the observed modifier key-down order when a complete
modifier-only action is committed on release. Releasing Meta before Shift
no longer turns Meta-then-Shift into Shift-then-Meta. Explicit modifier-only
Send to guest actions use Shift last. The 11 editable Host/browser defaults,
Guest overrides and first-release commit remain unchanged. Guest shortcuts
must match the guest's actual binding: forwarding Meta+Shift does not invoke
an Alt+Shift layout action. No extra input timer is introduced.
